  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front will bring widespread precipitation, and possibly \na rumble of thunder, to the area Wednesday night into Thursday. \nCurrently, precipitation amounts of half an inch to 2/3 of an \ninch seem likely. \n\nThe coldest air of the season will move in behind the front for\nFriday into the weekend. Higher elevations of the mountains \nshould begin to mix with and change over to snow Thursday \nnight. Precipitation on Friday will be enhanced by an upper \nlevel low crossing near the area. Behind the upper low, gusty\nwinds and cold air advection will create upslope snow \nconditions. The combination of these events could produce \nseveral inches of accumulating snow over the higher elevations \nof the mountains. Boundary layer temperatures will remain \nmarginal for snow in the lower elevation and the lowlands. If \nsnow does occur in those locations, ground temperatures should \nbe too warm for any accumulations.",
